        "heading": "1 INTRODUCTION",
        "text": "The modern paradigm for optimization of deep neural networks has practitioners working with vastly overparametrized models and training to near perfect accuracy (Zhang et al., 2017). In this setting, a model will typically have not just isolated minima in parameter space, but a continuous set of minimizers, not all of which generalize well. As demonstrated in Liu et al. (2020), depending on parameter initialization and hyperparameters, stochastic gradient descent (SGD) is capable of finding minima with wildly different test accuracies. Thus, the power of a particular optimization method lies in its ability to select a minima amongst this vast set that also generalizes. In other words, good generalization relies on the implicit bias or regularization of an optimization algorithm.\nThere is plenty of evidence that training deep nets with SGD leads to good generalization. Intuitively, SGD appears to prefer flatter minima (Keskar et al., 2017; Wu et al., 2018), and flatter minima are better for generalization (Hochreiter & Schmidhuber, 1997). More recently, a variant of SGD which introduces \u201calgorithmic\u201d label noise has been especially amenable to rigorous treatment. Indeed Blanc et al. (2020) were able to rigorously determine the implicit regularization of SGD with label noise in the phase of learning after training loss has converged to a minima. In the overparametrized setting, they show that SGD converges not just to any minimum, but to those minima that lead to the smallest trace norm of the Hessian. However, Li et al. (2022) show that the dynamics of this regularization happen on a timescale proportional to the inverse square of the learning rate \u03b7 \u2013 much slower than the time to converge to the zero loss manifold.\nWith the recent explosion in size of both models and datasets, training time becomes a question nearly as important as asymptotic generalization error. In this context, adaptive gradient methods such as Adam (Kingma & Ba, 2015) are unilaterally preferred over variants of SGD, even though they are often found to give worse generalization error in practical settings (Keskar & Socher, 2017; Wilson et al., 2017) (though extensive hyperparameter tuning Choi et al. (2019) or scheduling Xie et al. (2022) can potentially obviate this problem).\nThis motivates a search for methods to accelerate SGD. Classic work on acceleration methods, which we refer to generally as momentum, have found a provable benefit in the deterministic setting, where gradient updates have no error. However, rigorous guarantees have been harder to find in the stochastic setting, and when they do exist they tend to require strict conditions on the noise (Polyak, 1987; Kidambi et al., 2018).\nIn this work, we show that there exists a scaling limit for SGD with momentum (SGDM) which provably increases the rate of convergence."
