        "text": "Deep neural networks typically posses a manifold of parametrizations with zero training error. Because the gradients of the loss function vanish along this manifold, the dynamics of the weights is completely frozen under gradient descent. However, as appreciated by Wei & Schwab (2019) and Blanc et al. (2020), the effect of SGD noise can generate an average drift of the weights along the manifold, driving them to a lower-curvature region which, heuristically, explains the good generalization properties of SGD. Separately, it is well-known that adding momentum typically leads to acceleration in training Sutskever et al. (2013). We are interested in understanding the nature of this acceleration, and providing principled guidance for momentum hyperparameter tuning. In this section we will illustrate the main idea of this paper, showing how speedup towards flatter minima arises from the interaction of noise and momentum, and how this acceleration can be maximized by an appropriate hyperparameter scaling.\nWe model momentum SGD using the following equations\n\u03c0k+1 = \u03b2\u03c0k \u2212\u2207L(wk) + \u03c3(wk)\u03bek, wk+1 = wk + \u03b7\u03c0k+1, (1)\nwhere \u03c3(w) \u2208 RD\u00d7r is the noise function evaluated at w \u2208 RD, and \u03bek \u2208 Rr is a noise vector drawn i.i.d. at every timestep k with zero mean and unit variance. We will now form a heuristic understanding of the dynamics that happens along a manifold of minimizers \u0393 = {w : L(w) = 0}. Consider a point w \u2208 \u0393. The first non-zero order in the Taylor expansion of L at w will have a quadratic term given by the Hessian, H(w), which produces dynamics only in the space transverse to \u0393. This justifies assuming that the timescale associated to tangent displacements to \u0393, which we shall call \u03c42, is much longer than that associated to perpendicular displacements, \u03c41. More explicitly, denote the evolution of the weights near the zero-loss manifold aswk = w0+