        "text": "In this section we provide the proof of Theorem 3.4. Recall that \u03a6 satisfies\n\u03a6(x+ F (x)) = \u03a6(x) , (31)\nwhere x = (\u03c0,w), and F given in (114). To obtain the explicit expression of the limiting driftdiffusion, according to Theorem (B.4), and keeping into account Assumption (3.2), we need to determine \u03a6 up to its second derivatives. To this aim, we shall expand Eq. (31) up to second order in the series expansion in F . In components, this reads:\n\u03b7\u2202wi\u03a6 j(\u03c0i \u2212 gi) + \u2202\u03c0i\u03a6j(\u2212C\u03b7\u03b3\u03c0i \u2212 gi) +\n1 2 \u03b72\u2202wi\u2202wk\u03a6 j(\u03c0i \u2212 gi)(\u03c0k \u2212 gk)\n+ 1\n2 \u03b7\u2202wi\u2202\u03c0k\u03a6 j(\u03c0i \u2212 gi)(\u2212C\u03b7\u03b3\u03c0k \u2212 gk) + 1 2 \u03b7\u2202\u03c0i\u2202wk\u03a6 j(\u2212C\u03b7\u03b3\u03c0i \u2212 gi)(\u03c0k \u2212 gk)\n+ 1\n2 \u2202\u03c0i\u2202\u03c0k\u03a6\nj(\u2212C\u03b7\u03b3\u03c0i \u2212 gi)(\u2212C\u03b7\u03b3\u03c0k \u2212 gk) = 0\n(32)\nsubject to the boundary condition\n\u03a6(\u03c0,w)|w\u2208\u0393,\u03c0=0 = w . (33)\nHere, gi = \u2202iL. In Eq. (32), we already substituted \u03b2 = 1\u2212 C\u03b7\u03b3 . In what follows, we shall find \u03a6 to leading order in \u03b7 as \u03b7 \u2192 0. We will solve the above problem by performing a series expansion in \u03a6 around a point w\u0304 \u2208 \u0393 and \u03c0 = 0 up to second order:\n\u03a6(\u03c0, w\u0304+ \u03b4w) = \u03a600 + \u03a6 i 01\u03b4w i + \u03a6i10\u03c0 i +\n1 2 \u03a6ij02\u03b4w i\u03b4wj + \u03a6ij11\u03c0 i\u03b4wj + 1 2 \u03a6ij20\u03c0 i\u03c0j + \u00b7 \u00b7 \u00b7 . (34)\nFor example, we have\n\u03a6i01 = \u2202\u03a6\n\u2202wi , \u03a6ij11 =\n\u22022\u03a6\n\u2202\u03c0i\u2202wj , \u03a6ij20 =\n\u22022\u03a6\n\u2202\u03c0i\u2202\u03c0j . (35)\nMore precisely, we regard this as an expansion in powers of \u03b4w and \u03c0: \u03a600 is zeroth order, \u03a610,\u03a601 are first order, and the remaining terms are second order. We will occasionally write explicitly the index of \u03a6, e.g. \u03a6k,ij02 = \u2202\u03a6k \u2202wi\u2202wj . It will be useful to introduce the longitudinal projector onto \u0393,\nPL(w) : RD \u2192 Tw\u0393, defined such that for any vector v \u2208 Tw\u0393: PLv = v. The transverse projector is then PT = Id\u2212 PL. We will also decompose various tensors using these projectors, e.g.\n\u03a6ij11 = \u03a6 ij 11LL + \u03a6 ij 11LT + \u03a6 ij 11TL + \u03a6 ij 11TT , (36)\nwith \u03a6ij11LT = \u03a6 kl 11P ik L P jl T . Note that the Hessian H = \u2207g \u2208 RD\u00d7D satisfies HH\u2020 = PT , where H\u2020 denotes the pseudoinverse.\nAt zeroth order, we obviously have \u03a600(w) = w. Lemma C.1. The first order terms in the series expansion (34) are given by\n\u03a601T = \u03a610T = 0, \u03a601L = PL, \u03a610L = C \u22121\u03b71\u2212\u03b3PL . (37)\nProof. We now proceed to finding the first order terms. Suppose w\u0302(s) \u2208 RD is a curve lying on \u0393. Then due to the boundary condition (33), \u2202s\u03a6(\u03c0 = 0, w\u0302) = \u03a6i01\u2202sw\u0302 i = \u2202sw\u0302 i. This means that \u03a601L = PL. Now from (32), \u03b7\u03a6i01(\u03c0\ni \u2212 \u2202jgi\u03b4wj) + \u03a6i10(\u2212C\u03b7\u03b3\u03c0i \u2212 \u2202jgi\u03b4wj) = 0 . (38) This condition should hold for any \u03c0 and \u03b4w, therefore we arrive at\n\u03a610 = C \u22121\u03b71\u2212\u03b3\u03a601 (39)\nand (\u03b7\u03a6i01 + \u03a6 i 10)\u2202kg\ni = 0 . (40) Decomposing into longitudinal and transverse components, and noting that the Hessian satisfies PLH = 0, the above equation becomes\n\u03b7\u03a6i01T + \u03a6 i 10T = 0 , (41)\nwhich together with (39) and the above discussion gives \u03a601T = \u03a610T = 0, \u03a601L = PL, \u03a610L = C\n\u22121\u03b71\u2212\u03b3PL , (42) which concludes the first order analysis.\nWe now need a Lemma, which requires Definition (3.3) in the main text. We report it here for convenience: Definition C.2. For a symmetric matrix H \u2208 RD \u00d7 RD, and WH = {\u03a3 \u2208 RD \u00d7 RD : \u03a3 = \u03a3> , HH\u2020\u03a3 = H\u2020H\u03a3 = \u03c3}, we define the operator L\u0303H : WH \u2192 WH with L\u0303HS \u2261 {H,S} + 1 2C \u22122\u03b71\u22122\u03b3 [[S,H], H], with [S,H] = SH \u2212HS. Lemma C.3. The inverse of the operator L\u0303H is unique.\nProof. Let us go to a basis where H is diagonal, with eigenvalues \u03bbi. In components, the equation L\u0303HS = M reads\n(\u03bbi + \u03bbj)Sij + 1\n2 C\u22122\u03b71\u22122\u03b3(\u03bbi \u2212 \u03bbj)2Sij + C\u22121\u03b71\u2212\u03b3Mij = 0 (43)\nwhich has a unique solution, with Sij = \u2212C\u22121\u03b71\u2212\u03b3 ( \u03bbi + \u03bbj + 1\n2 C\u22122\u03b71\u22122\u03b3(\u03bbi \u2212 \u03bbj)2\n)\u22121 Mij . (44)\nLemma C.4. The second order terms in the series expansion (34) are given by\n\u03a6j,ik02LL = \u2212(H \u2020)jl\u2202Li H lnPnkL , \u03a6 j,ik 02LT = \u2212P jl L \u2202 L i H ln(H\u2020)nk, \u03a6j,ik02TT = O ( \u03b7min{0,1\u22122\u03b3} ) (45)\n\u03a6j,ik11LL = \u2212C \u22121\u03b71\u2212\u03b3(H\u2020)jl\u2202Li H lnPnkL , \u03a6 j,ik 11TL = \u2212C \u22121\u03b71\u2212\u03b3P jlL \u2202 L kH ln(H\u2020)ni (46)\n\u03a6j,ik11TT = \u2212C \u22121\u03b71\u2212\u03b3L\u0303\u22121H (M (j))ik \u2212 1 2 C\u22123\u03b72\u22123\u03b3 [H, L\u0303\u22121H M (j)]ik (47) \u03a6j,ik20LL = \u2212 1\n2 C\u22122\u03b72\u22122\u03b3\n( (H\u2020)jl\u2202Li H lnPnkL + (H \u2020)jl\u2202LkH lnPniL )\n(48)\n\u03a6j,ik20TL = \u2212 1 2 C\u22122\u03b72\u22122\u03b3\n( P jlL \u2202 L kH ln(H\u2020)ni + P jlL \u2202 L i H ln(H\u2020)nk )\n(49)\n\u03a6j,ik20TT = \u2212C \u22122\u03b72\u22122\u03b3L\u0303\u22121H (PT\u2202 L j HPT )ik (50)\nwhere (M (j))kl = P ki T \u2202 L j HniP nl T (51)\nProof. Consider a path w\u0302(s) lying on \u0393. From PTH = H we have\ndPT ds H = (Id\u2212 PT ) dH ds = PL dH ds , (52)\nand thus, using HH\u2020 = PT , we find\ndPT ds PT = PL dH ds H\u2020, PT dPT ds = H\u2020 dH ds PL , (53)\nwhere the second equation is obtained from the first by taking the transpose. Putting the last two relations together, we find\ndPL ds = \u2212dPT ds = \u2212PT dPT ds \u2212 dPT ds PT = \u2212H\u2020 dH ds PL \u2212 PL dH ds H\u2020 . (54)\nFrom (37) we can then write\n\u2202Li \u03a6 j,k 01 = \u2212(H\u2020)jl\u2202Li H lnPnkL \u2212 P jl L \u2202 L i H ln(H\u2020)nk , (55)\nwhere \u2202Li = P ij L \u2202j . This leads to\n\u03a6j,ik02LL = P kl L \u2202 L i \u03a6 j,l 01 = \u2212(H\u2020)jl\u2202Li H lnPnkL (56)\n\u03a6j,ik02LT = P kl T \u2202 L i \u03a6 j,l 01 = \u2212P jl L \u2202 L i H ln(H\u2020)nk . (57)\nAlso note that \u03a6j,ik02LT = \u03a6 j,ki 02TL, so the only component still to be determined in \u03a602 is \u03a602TT .\nThe next step is to expand Eq. (32) to second order: \u03c0i\u03c0k ( \u03b7(1 + C\u03b7\u03b3)\u03a6ki11 \u2212 C\u03b7\u03b3(1\u2212 12C\u03b7 \u03b3)\u03a6ik20 + 1 2\u03b7 2\u03a6ik02 )\n+\u03b4wk\u03c0i ( \u03b7\u03a6ik02 \u2212 \u03b72Hkj\u03a6 ji 02 \u2212 \u03b7\u03a6il11Hkl \u2212 C\u03b7\u03b3\u03a6ik11 \u2212 \u03b7(1\u2212C\u03b7\u03b3)\u03a6 (ij) 11 Hkj \u2212 (1\u2212C\u03b7\u03b3)\u03a6li20Hkl ) \u2212\u03b4wk\u03b4wl ( \u03b7\u03a6ik02Hli + 1 2\u03b7 2\u03a6ji02HljHki + \u03a6 ik 11Hli + \u03b7\u03a6 ji 11HkjHli\n+ 12 (\u03b7\u03a6 i 01 + \u03a6 i 10)\u2202kHli + 1 2\u03a6 ji 20HljHki\n) = 0 ,\n(58)\nwhere A(ij) = 12 (A ij +Aji) denotes the symmetric part. Neglecting various terms that are subleading at small \u03b7, gives\n\u03c0i\u03c0k ( \u03b7\u03a6ki11 \u2212 C\u03b7\u03b3\u03a6ik20 + 12\u03b7 2\u03a6ik02 )\n+\u03b4wk\u03c0i ( \u03b7\u03a6ik02 \u2212 C\u03b7\u03b3\u03a6ik11 \u2212 \u03a6li20Hkl ) \u2212\u03b4wk\u03b4wl ( \u03b7\u03a6ik02Hli + \u03a6 ik 11Hli + 1 2 (\u03b7\u03a6 i 01 + \u03a6 i 10)\u2202kHli + 1 2\u03a6 ji 20HljHki ) = 0 ,\n(59)\nThe first line immediately gives\n\u03a6ij20 = C \u22121\u03b71\u2212\u03b3\u03a6 (ij) 11 +\n1 2 C\u22121\u03b72\u2212\u03b3\u03a6ij02 . (60)\nTaking the second line of (59) and projecting onto the longitudinal part the index k gives\n\u03b7\u03b2\u03a6ik02LL \u2212 C\u03b7\u03b3\u03a6ik11LL = 0 (61) \u03b7\u03b2\u03a6ik02TL \u2212 C\u03b7\u03b3\u03a6ik11TL = 0 (62)\nand using (56),(57),\n\u03a6j,ik11LL = C \u22121\u03b71\u2212\u03b3\u03a6j,ik02LL = \u2212C \u22121\u03b71\u2212\u03b3(H\u2020)jl\u2202Li H lnPnkL (63) \u03a6j,ik11TL = C \u22121\u03b71\u2212\u03b3\u03a6j,ik02TL = \u2212C \u22121\u03b71\u2212\u03b3P jlL \u2202 L kH ln(H\u2020)ni (64)\nProjecting the second line of (59) on the transverse part of the index k we have\n\u03b7\u03a6j,ik02LT \u2212 C\u03b7 \u03b3\u03a6j,ik11LT \u2212 \u03a6 j,li 20TLHkl = 0 (65)\n\u03b7\u03a6j,ik02TT \u2212 C\u03b7 \u03b3\u03a6j,ik11TT \u2212 \u03a6 j,li 20TTHkl = 0 (66)\nUsing (60) and keeping (57) into account, Eq. (65) becomes, neglecting subleading terms in \u03b7,\n\u2212\u03b7P jlL \u2202 L i H ln(H\u2020)nk \u2212 C\u03b7\u03b3\u03a6ik11LT \u2212 1 2 C\u22121\u03b71\u2212\u03b3\u03a6li11TLHkl \u2212 1 2 C\u22121\u03b71\u2212\u03b3\u03a6il11LTHkl = 0 .\n(67)\nUsing (64) in (67),\n\u2212\u03b7P jlL \u2202 L i H ln(H\u2020)nk \u2212 C\u03b7\u03b3\u03a6ik11LT\n+ 1\n2 C\u22122\u03b72\u22122\u03b3P jpL \u2202 L i H\npn(H\u2020)nlHkl \u2212 1\n2 C\u22121\u03b71\u2212\u03b3\u03a6il11LTHkl = 0 ,\n(68)\nand simplifying,\n\u2212\u03b7P jlL \u2202 L i H ln(H\u2020)nk \u2212 C\u03b7\u03b3\u03a6ik11LT\n+ 1\n2 C\u22122\u03b72\u22122\u03b3P jlL \u2202 L i H\nlnPnkT \u2212 1\n2 C\u22121\u03b71\u2212\u03b3\u03a6il11LTHkl = 0 .\n(69)\nwhich determines \u03a611LT in close form. Indeed, the above has the form, in matrix notation\n\u2212 1 2 C\u22121\u03b71\u2212\u03b3\u03a611LTH \u2212 C\u03b7\u03b3\u03a611LT = M , (70)\nand can be immediately inverted to solve for \u03a611LT . The only two undetermined components now are \u03a611TT and \u03a602TT . One condition is obtained from (66) which gives, keeping (60) into account,\n\u03b7\u03a6j,ik02TT \u2212 C\u03b7 \u03b3\u03a6j,ik11TT \u2212 C \u22121\u03b71\u2212\u03b3\u03a6 j,(li) 11TTHkl = 0 , (71)\nand thus \u03a6j,ik02TT = C\u03b7 \u03b3\u22121\u03a6j,ik11TT + C \u22121\u03b7\u2212\u03b3\u03a6 j,(ni) 11TTHkn . (72) Further taking symmetric and antisymmetric part in ik of the above gives\n\u03a6j,ik02TT =C\u03b7 \u03b3\u22121\u03a6 j,(ik) 11TT +\n1 2 C\u22121\u03b7\u2212\u03b3\u03a6 j,(ni) 11TTHkn + 1 2 C\u22121\u03b7\u2212\u03b3\u03a6 j,(nk) 11TT Hin (73)\n0 =C\u03b7\u03b3\u22121\u03a6 j,[ik] 11TT +\n1 2 C\u22121\u03b7\u2212\u03b3\u03a6 j,(ni) 11TTHkn \u2212 1 2 C\u22121\u03b7\u2212\u03b3\u03a6 j,(nk) 11TT Hin . (74)\nThe other condition comes from the third line of (59) which, using (37) and (60), and neglecting subleading terms in \u03b7, gives\n\u03b7\u03a6j,ik02 Hli + \u03b7\u03a6 j,il 02 Hki + \u03a6 j,ik 11 Hli + \u03a6 j,il 11 Hki + C \u22121\u03b71\u2212\u03b3P jiL \u2202kHli = 0 (75)\nProjecting the k and l indices on the longitudinal part, gives P jiL \u2202 L kHliP ln L = 0, which is an identity that can be checked from (52). Projecting k on the longitudinal part and l on the transverse part gives\n\u03b7\u03a6j,ik02TLHli + \u03a6 j,ik 11TLHli + C \u22121\u03b71\u2212\u03b3P jiL \u2202 L kHniP nl T = 0 , (76)\nwhich is implied by (64), indeed\n(\u03b7\u03a6j,ik02TL + \u03a6 j,ik 11TL)Hli = C \u22121\u03b71\u2212\u03b3\u03a6j,ik02TLHli\n= \u2212C\u22121\u03b71\u2212\u03b3P jlL \u2202 L kH ln(H\u2020)niHli = \u2212C\u22121\u03b71\u2212\u03b3P jlL \u2202 L kH lnPnlT , (77)\ntherefore (76) does not give a new condition. The only new condition comes from projecting the k and l indices of (75) on the transverse direction, giving\n\u03b7\u03a6j,ik02TTHli + \u03b7\u03a6 j,il 02TTHki + \u03a6 j,ik 11TTHli + \u03a6 j,il 11TTHki + C \u22121\u03b71\u2212\u03b3P jiL \u2202 T k HniP nl T = 0 . (78)\nPlugging in (72),\nC\u03b7\u03b3\u03a6j,ik11TTHli + C \u22121\u03b71\u2212\u03b3\u03a6 j,(in) 11TTHknHli + C\u03b7 \u03b3\u03a6j,il11TTHki + C \u22121\u03b71\u2212\u03b3\u03a6 j,(in) 11TTHlnHki\n+\u03a6j,ik11TTHli + \u03a6 j,il 11TTHki + C \u22121\u03b71\u2212\u03b3P jiL \